{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob04re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob04re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob04re2img01.jpg", "text": "Before we go deeper into the operators topic, there are some things that have to be cleared out beforehand. In a formula, there are two actors: operands which are the differential factors, and the operators which define the interaction between operands. There are different types of operators people can pick out from to perform a given action in JavaScript. Such operators can be used to perform different operations as in mathematics. Let's check them out.", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "The arithmetic operators offer a variety of operations which include \u2018addition', \u2018subtraction', \u2018multiplication', \u2018division', and \u2018modules', among others. Those operators use a direct arithmetic interaction between operands. The comparison operators offer \u2018equal', \u2018not equal', \u2018greater than', and \u2018less than' operations and their possible combinations. These operators can be used to check interaction and differences in values between operands. The logical operators like \u2018logical AND', \u2018logical OR', and \u2018logical NOT', have to be used as conditional sentences where a given result depends on a specific condition. The bitwise operators which are \u2018bitwise AND', \u2018bitwise OR', \u2018bitwise XOR', \u2018left shift', \u2018right shift', etc., have to be used to perform bit-by-bit operations where values are manipulated to calculate and compare by means of binary numerals. Assignment operators that comprise the \u2018simple assignment', \u2018add and assignment', \u2018subtract and assignment', and others, assign a value to the left operand taking into account the value of the right operand. And the last two which are, on the one hand,  the conditional operator that evaluates whether a statement is true or false, and then executes one of these two statements depending on the result; and on the other hand, the \u201ctypeof\u201d operator that indicates the data type of the operand.", "time": "35000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob04re2img03.jpg", "text": "It is always a good idea to count on a reference chart if you're new at JavaScript. In case you can't get the expected output, you can rely on online tutorials or an experienced person to get you out of trouble. Make sure your spelling is correct to avoid typos and misplaced symbols. This will save you a lot of time instead of having to work something over.", "time": "141500"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can say what an operator is in JavaScript.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can list different types of operators in JavaScript. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about characteristics of different types of operators in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How do you think operators in JavaScript are used? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What types of operators do you know and what are they used for? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your part